-\section{The quantized-circulation branch and the circulation deficit}
-\label{sec:quantum}
-
-\subsection{Cancellation of \texorpdfstring{$\alpha$}{alpha} and a parameter-free density}
-
-In superfluids, circulation about a vortex line is quantized in units of $h/m$, with $m$ the mass of the circulating constituent \cite{Onsager1949,Feynman1955,Vinen1961}.  If the filament of Scheme~C carries one full quantum $\Gamma_C=h/m_e$ and the predicted length \cref{eq:ellstar}, the closure \cref{eq:schemeC} fixes the density with no reference to $\alpha$:
-\begin{equation}
-  \rho_C=\frac{3m_ec^2}{\Gamma_C^{2}\,\ell_*}
-  =\frac{8\pi m_ec^2}{\Gamma_C^{2}\lC}
-  =\boxed{\ \frac{2}{\pi}\,\frac{m_e}{\lC^{3}}
-  =1.007\,09\times10^{7}\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}\ }.
+Thus the agreement of any one representation cannot be used as independent corroboration of the other two.
+
+\subsection{Electromagnetic self-energy matching}
+
+A statement such as \(D=\re\) is not a geometry theorem.  The electromagnetic self-energy of a finite charge distribution has the general form
+\begin{equation}
+  E_{\rm EM}
+  =
+  C_{\rm EM}
+  \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0D},
+  \label{eq:EM-shape}
+\end{equation}
+where \(C_{\rm EM}\) depends on the charge profile, topology, screening, and boundary conditions.  Requiring \(E_{\rm EM}=m_ec^2\) yields
+\begin{equation}
+  D=C_{\rm EM}\re.
+  \label{eq:shape-match}
+\end{equation}
+Therefore \(D=\re\) follows only for the special convention \(C_{\rm EM}=1\).  For a knotted or toroidal charge distribution, \(C_{\rm EM}\) must be computed.
+
+It is therefore more accurate to call
+\[
+E_{\rm hydro}=E_{\rm EM}=m_ec^2
+\]
+an \emph{equal-self-energy matching ansatz}, not equipartition.
+
+\section{The Compton-circulation comparison branch}
+\label{sec:compton-branch}
+
+\subsection{An \texorpdfstring{\(\alpha\)-free}{alpha-free}, electron-anchored density scale}
+
+Using \(\GammaC=h/m_e=2\pi\lC c\) in the filament closure and
+\(\ell_*=(3/8\pi)\lC\) gives
+\begin{align}
+  \rhoCeff
+  &=
+  \frac{3m_ec^2}{\GammaC^2\ell_*}
+  \notag\\
+  &=
+  \frac{8\pi m_ec^2}{\GammaC^2\lC}
+  =
+  \boxed{
+  \frac{2}{\pi}\frac{m_e}{\lC^3}
+  }
+  =
+  1.0071\times10^7\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}.
   \label{eq:rhoC}
-\end{equation}
-Every power of $\alpha$ cancels identically: $\ell_*$ is $\alpha$-free by construction, and $\Gamma_C$ contains only $h$ and $m_e$.  The closed form is memorable --- up to the factor $2/\pi$, one electron mass per cubic reduced Compton wavelength --- and its derivation chain contains only $\{\hbar,m_e,c\}$ together with the declared normalizations.  Equivalently, the two closures $E=m_ec^2$ and $\ell_*=(3/8\pi)\lC$ combine into the single identity
-\begin{equation}
-  \rho_C\,\Gamma_C^{2}\,\lC=8\pi\,m_ec^{2},
-  \label{eq:master-identity}
-\end{equation}
-verified numerically to machine precision ($2.0576\times10^{-12}$~J on both sides).
-
-An important structural consequence follows.  Because $\alpha$ cancels in \cref{eq:master-identity}, \emph{this class of closures cannot determine $\alpha$}: given the geometry, the pair of bridges (hydrodynamic energy assignment; electromagnetic comparison) yields exactly one equation, and that equation determines the medium density, not the coupling.  Conversely, an independent determination of $\rho$ would, through the same identity, fix $\ell_*$, hence $D_{\rm geom}$, hence the predicted coupling.  The medium density is thus the model's single genuine unknown.
-
-\subsection{The superluminal obstruction}
-
-The branch \cref{eq:rhoC} cannot be read as a literal incompressible flow.  The azimuthal speed about a line vortex of circulation $\Gamma_C$ is $v(r)=\Gamma_C/(2\pi r)=c\,\lC/r$, which reaches $c$ at $r=\lC$ and exceeds it everywhere inside.  The energy-bearing region of the closure lies entirely at sub-Compton radii: at the core radius $a=D_{\rm geom}/2$ the formal speed is $274c$ $(=2c/\alpha)$, and even at the outer scale $\sim2\ell_*$ it is $4.2c$.  This is the vortex analogue of the superluminal-surface-speed objection to classical spinning-electron models \cite{Ohanian1986}.  Consequently Scheme~C is admissible only as dimensional bookkeeping: either the medium's signal speed is not $c$ at these scales (in which case the emergent-relativity problem must be confronted on its own terms \cite{BarceloLiberatiVisser2026}), or the effective circulation at sub-Compton scales is smaller than the quantum.
-
-\subsection{The circulation deficit}
-
-The subluminal Scheme~B corresponds to the tangential speed $v=\alpha c/2$ at the core radius, hence to the effective circulation
-\begin{equation}
-  \Gamma_{\rm eff}=2\pi r v
-  =2\pi\cdot\frac{\alpha\lC}{2}\cdot\frac{\alpha c}{2}
-  =\frac{\alpha^{2}}{4}\,\frac{h}{m_e},
-  \qquad
-  \frac{\Gamma_{\rm eff}}{\Gamma_C}=\frac{\alpha^{2}}{4}
-  =1.3313\times10^{-5},
-  \label{eq:deficit}
-\end{equation}
-and accordingly to a density larger than \cref{eq:rhoC} by $(\Gamma_C/\Gamma_{\rm eff})^{2}=16/\alpha^{4}=5.6423\times10^{9}$; numerically $\rho_B/\rho_C=5.6423\times10^{9}$, in exact agreement.  \Cref{eq:densities} is thereby fully accounted for: the eleven orders of magnitude decompose as one exact volume ratio, \cref{eq:ratio-value}, times one exact circulation ratio squared.
-
-We regard \cref{eq:deficit} as the sharpest formulation of what such a model does not yet explain.  A quantized medium naturally supplies $\Gamma_C=h/m_e$; the subluminal branch requires that the circulation actually stored at sub-Compton scales be suppressed by exactly $\alpha^{2}/4$.  No mechanism for this suppression is proposed here.  Any dynamical account of the electron along these lines must either derive the deficit or replace the incompressible description below $\lC$.
+\end{align}
+This expression is independent of \(\alpha\), but it is not parameter free in the predictive sense: it uses the measured \(m_e\), \(\hbar\), \(c\), the geometric coefficient \(3/(8\pi)\), and the line-energy coefficient \(1/3\).  It is an electron-anchored density scale, not a derived substrate density.
+
+Within this frozen closure, \(\alpha\) cancels and the algebra determines a density coefficient rather than the coupling.  This is a structural statement about the closure, not a no-go theorem for every possible SST derivation of \(\alpha\).
+
+\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Formal speed relative to \(c\)}{Formal speed relative to c}}
+
+For a line vortex with \(\GammaC=2\pi\lC c\),
+\begin{equation}
+  v_\theta(r)
+  =
+  \frac{\GammaC}{2\pi r}
+  =
+  c\frac{\lC}{r}.
+  \label{eq:vGammaC}
+\end{equation}
+At \(a=D_{\rm geom}/2\), the formal ratio is
+\[
+\frac{v_\theta(a)}{c}
+=
+\frac{2}{\alpha_{\rm geom}}
+\simeq274.3,
+\]
+and at \(r=2\ell_*\),
+\[
+\frac{v_\theta(2\ell_*)}{c}
+=
+\frac{4\pi}{3}
+\simeq4.19.
+\]
+
+These numbers do not contradict Galilean incompressible Euler theory, in which \(c\) is not a fundamental speed limit and the pressure constraint is instantaneous.  They do obstruct a literal identification of this azimuthal transport speed with a physical matter velocity in a sector obeying universal operational Lorentz invariance with limiting speed \(c\).  A viable model must therefore supply a separate emergent-causality map, a finite-core modification of \cref{eq:vGammaC}, or a different circulation sector \cite{BarceloLiberatiVisser2026}.
+
+\subsection{Calibrated circulation ratio}
+
+On the calibrated horn chain,
+\begin{equation}
+  r=\frac{\alpha}{2}\lC,
+  \qquad
+  v=\frac{\alpha}{2}c,
+\end{equation}
+so
+\begin{equation}
